    {
      "filename": "Family (16900196392).jpg",
      "title": "File:Family (16900196392).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/00/Family_%2816900196392%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Family_(16900196392).jpg",
      "width": 4256,
      "height": 2832,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Chris Hunkeler from Carlsbad, California, USA",
      "description": "The little boy in red is having a merry old time skipping down the dune until he falls on his face moment's later. He's a trooper through and the sand is soft enough so the fall does not faze him in the least.\nWhile not a desert at all, Carcross Desert, as the locals are inclined to call it, is actually a large deposit of sand on a glacial lake bed. The sand is blown from the beach at Bennett Lake about 2 km away when the water level is low exposing the large sandy beach. The beach is formed from silt and sand deposited as the Watson River empties into the lake.\nFor more information on this fascinating 260 hectare (642 acre) sand dunes area, visit The Basement Geographer's article, The Carcross Desert. \n\nQUS_2746",
      "dateCreated": "2014-06-26 14:26",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "carcross-desert-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "c48n"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Mountain Dunes (16281426293).jpg",
      "title": "File:Mountain Dunes (16281426293).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/Mountain_Dunes_%2816281426293%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mountain_Dunes_(16281426293).jpg",
      "width": 4256,
      "height": 2832,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Chris Hunkeler from Carlsbad, California, USA",
      "description": "Roses (Rosa acicularis) grace the top of this dune in the foreground as the main part of the dune rises beyond to a majestic mountain in the background.\nThis area gets double the rainfall than would be necessary to designate it a desert but less than the surrounding areas.\nWhile not a desert, Carcross Desert, as the locals call it, is actually a large deposit of sand on a 10,000 year old glacial lake bed. The sand is blown from the beach at Bennett Lake about 2 km away when the water level is low exposing the large sandy beach. The beach is formed from silt and sand deposited as the Watson River empties into the lake.\nFor more information on this fascinating 260 hectare (642 acre) sand dunes area, visit The Basement Geographer's article, The Carcross Desert. \n\nQUS_2770-1",
      "dateCreated": "2014-06-26 14:36",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "carcross-desert-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "c48n"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Trembling Aspen and White Spruce (16715284539).jpg",
      "title": "File:Trembling Aspen and White Spruce (16715284539).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Trembling_Aspen_and_White_Spruce_%2816715284539%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trembling_Aspen_and_White_Spruce_(16715284539).jpg",
      "width": 4256,
      "height": 2832,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Chris Hunkeler from Carlsbad, California, USA",
      "description": "As the dunes creep ever higher, they overtake the trunks of Trembling Aspen leaving the root flare/collar buried meters/yards below the surface.\nWhile not a desert at all, Carcross Desert, as the locals are inclined to call it, is actually a large deposit of sand on a glacial lake bed. The sand is blown from the beach at Bennett Lake about 2 km away when the water level is low exposing the large sandy beach. The beach is formed from silt and sand deposited as the Watson River empties into the lake.\nFor more information on this fascinating 260 hectare (642 acre) sand dunes area, visit The Basement Geographer's article, The Carcross Desert. \n\nQUS_2848",
      "dateCreated": "2014-06-26 14:59",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "carcross-desert-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "c48n"
    },
